ted his floor from the low 30s to 38 percent, but that only narrows the margin, not the likely outcome.", "watch": "A late affordability-focused surge or heavy Minogue spending could tighten the margin, though it would need to move well beyond anything current polls show to threaten the result.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Incumbent Maura Healey (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Massachusetts governor is one of the safest Democratic holds on the 2026 map. Incumbent Maura Healey has led Republican Mike Minogue in every poll of the race, most recently 54-38 in a late-September University of New Hampshire survey of likely voters. All six raters place the contest in the Solid or Safe Democratic column, and prediction markets price Healey's win chances around 95 percent or better.\n\nMinogue, who won the September primary, has campaigned on affordability and auditing Beacon Hill, and both nominees back a gas tax holiday. Those themes have not dented Healey's standing in a state with a 14-point Democratic lean, and an independent candidate, Andrea James, is on the ballot without competing for the lead. The main question is the size of Healey's victory margin, not which party wins.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9961, "margin": 18.83}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9968, "margin": 20.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9983, "margin": 21.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9947, "margin": 17.5}]}